This is pretty spoily.
Yep.
Spoily, spoily, spoily.
But not totally.

Others have already chimed in with a few methods
of rust removal. They're all useful to know in various
situations. (I s'pose I'll chip in that, in SLASH'EM,
some shopkeepers offer damage-proofing services.)

In this case, I agree with whoever said you should #dip
your sword into fountains, especially now that it's
already rusty. Keep trying. As noted, this particular
method only works for Lawfuls, and it works best
for knights.

Also note that a katana uses the long sword skill
and is slightly better than a long sword of equal
enchantment. They're sort of rare, but you never
know when you'll stumble across one. (If it isn't
identified, it'll show up as a "samurai sword".)
If you've one handy, you might switch over to it.

Sacrificing corpses at an altar is another fine way
to go about things, if you have an altar handy. That
'forbidding feeling' you got in the minetown temple
indeed means that sacrificing to it would be a Bad
Thing - the resident priest'll get all offended. You can
still drop items on it safely, though, to learn their
status. (A co-aligned temple would give you a
peaceful feeling.)

I believe that Odin is the lawful Valk diety, so an altar
to Odin is ideal. Other altars can be converted, but
remember:

a) It might take a few tries.

b) Don't even -try- with an altar to Moloch, or a high
altar. Bad idea. You won't run into these for a while.

c) If your luck is shot to hell or Odin is pissed off at
you, you might end up being converted to the altar's
alignment, instead. So it's a bit of a risk, especially if
you aren't familiar with manipulating your luck*.

(Don't change your alignment on purpose until you're
sure you know what you're doing. Early in the game, it
can easily make the game unwinnable. This is, AFAIK,
the -only- way to make the game unwinnable other than
by dying or escaping the dungeon.)

d) Don't use unicorn corpses to convert an altar. For
that matter, never sacrifice a white unicorn on a lawful
altar, grey on a neutral altar, or black on a chaotic one.

e) Unless you're chaotic, don't sacrifice corpses of the
same race as yourself.

f) Never sacrifice former pets.

g) I'm probably forgetting some useful stuff here.
Also, there's a lot of things you shouldn't do at
an altar that should be obvious. Don't kick it, for
example.

Also note that, as a Valkyrie, your first gift will always
be Mjollnir, which does lots of damage but tends to
blow up wands and rings in the opponent's inventory.
If you get strong enough, it will return when thrown, and
occasionally blow up wands and rings in your inventory.

My first ascension was a Valk with Mjollnir - it's a
strong but annoying weapon. It's certainly a step up
from a +1 longsword, but keep in the back of your
mind that you might get sick and tired of it, so keep
your eyes open for alternatives if you do get it.

Meanwhile, if you have or find a can of grease, use
it on anything and everything that looks rust-able and
is important. Throughout the game I often keep my
cloak, bags, helmet, and stethoscope greased, for
reasons that will probably become apparent over the
course of time. An early can of grease, though, gets
all over -everything-. I don't even care if it's cursed, if
I have a towel lying around.


